# TWO COPIES OF THE SAME LATTICE KNOT, PROJECTING EXACTLY ONTO EACH OTHER.
# lattice_04 and a copy translated by (0,0,10) -- only in z, so the two
# projections are IDENTICAL, vertex for vertex and edge for edge.
#
# Where split_lattice puts two lattice knots side by side and asks that nothing
# leak between them, this one superimposes them and asks that everything be
# separated. Every degeneracy of a lattice projection is now doubled on top of
# itself: max_point_multiplicity goes from 8 to 16, collinear_overlap from 78 to
# 202, coincident_vertices from 192 to 448. Every edge of one copy projects onto
# exactly the corresponding edge of the other.
#
# Simulation of simplicity handles it, and the stability ladder is the
# interesting part: lattice_04 alone stabilises at N = 8, this needs N = 64 --
# a finer perturbation, because there is far more coincident structure to pull
# apart. 88 crossings, and all three classes agree at all three coordinate
# types.
#
# 88 = 19 + 19 + 50: each copy's own crossings, plus 50 between the copies.
#
# THE LINK TYPE IS THE SPLIT UNION, and that is checkable rather than asserted:
# the copies occupy z in [1,4] and [11,14], so the plane z = 7.5 separates them
# and they cannot be linked. The computed diagram agrees -- all 50
# inter-component crossings sum to linking number 0. It is the same link as
# split_lattice, reached through an 88-crossing diagram instead of a 38-crossing
# one.
#
# lattice_04 is the UNKNOT: HOMFLY = 1 on its own 19-crossing diagram, against
# the trefoil's -L^-4 - 2L^-2 + L^-2 M^2, measured side by side with the
# vendored oracle. So both fixtures are the 2-component unlink, and
# PlanarDiagramComplex::Simplify takes both all the way down: TWO PRIME
# SUMMANDS, EACH ONE CIRCLE WITH NO CROSSINGS AT ALL -- not two 3-crossing
# pieces, and not merely two pieces that fail to cross each other. Each
# component is individually crossingless. HOMFLY is preserved, as it must be.
# Verified by calling Simplify on the object rather than reading the CLI's
# text output. Getting 88 crossings down to 0 while keeping the components apart is
# the real work here, and it is worth knowing that the whole 88 is manufactured
# by the projection rather than by the topology.
#
# Regenerate:
#   python3 - <<'EOF'
#   rows=[l.split() for l in open('lattice_04.crd') if l.strip()]
#   DZ=10
#   out=[" ".join(r) for r in rows] + [""] + [f"{r[0]} {r[1]} {int(r[2])+DZ}" for r in rows]
#   open('stacked_lattice.crd','w').write("\n".join(out)+"\n")
#   EOF
note = two identical lattice knots superimposed in projection; 88 crossings, linking number 0
knot = 2-component unlink (lattice_04 is unknotted; HOMFLY = 1)

vertical_edges         == 36
collinear_overlap      == 202
coincident_vertices    == 448
multiple_points        == 16
max_point_multiplicity == 16
spatial                == 0
